 { We are familiar with such positions from Chapter Three, where the stronger side usually undertook a king transfer to the queenside. However, Anand is about to show us that, in such a kingside structure with a weak pawn on g5, a completely different plan is available: the transposition to a pawn ending. } 1. { -KEY- } a7 $1 Kh7 2. Ke2 Kg7 3. Kd3 Kh7 4. Kc3 Kg7 5. Kb3 Ra1 6. Kc4 Ra5 7. Kb4 Ra1 8. Kb5 Rb1+ 9. Kc6 Ra1 10. Kd6 Ra6+ 11. Kc7 Ra2 12. Kb6 Rb2+ 13. Kc6 $1 Ra2 ( { After } 13... Rc2 14. Kd6 $1 Ra2 ( { a further rear attack with } 14... Rd2 15. Ke6 Re2 { fails to } 16. Kf5 ) 
15. Rc8 $1 ( { not } 14... Ra2 14. Rd8 $2 Ra6 $1 { a move which can now be met by Rc6 } ) 
Rxa7 16. Rc7 Rxc7 17. Kxc7 { White wins in similar fashion - Golubev. } ) 
14. Rd8 $1 { The pawn is abandoned in order to force the exchange of rooks, after which the weak g5-pawn will decide matters. } Rxa7 15. Rd7+ Rxd7 16. Kxd7 Kf7 17. Kd6 ( { Also good was } 17. f3 Kf6 18. Kd6 { etc. } ) 
Kf6 18. Kd5 f3 19. Kd6 Kf7 ( { Black resigned at the same time, without waiting for } 19... Kf7 20. Ke5 ) 

 1-0

