 { Obviously, the simplifying measures don't solely have to produce pawn endings in order to be effective. Here are two examples where more pieces remained on the board after the simplification.To facilitate the winning process, Karpov starts looking for the exchange of rooks. } 1. { -KEY- } Qd4 $1 { Tactics at the service of strategy. } Rde8 ( { a) } 1... Ree8 { obviously failed to } 2. Qxb6 ) 
 ( { b) } 1... Rf8 { while after } 2. Qd7 Ree8 3. Qb5 Re6 4. Rc6 $1 { , White would reach his goal anyway. } ) 
2. Qd7 Ng4 3. Rc8 Nf6 ( { Or } 3... Re1 4. Kg2 R1e2 5. Kh3 Nf2 6. Kh4 R2e4 7. g4 Rxg4 8. Qxg4 { and wins - Karpov. } ) 
4. Rxe8+ Rxe8 5. Qb7 Re6 6. Qb8+ Ne8 7. a4 { White starts working on his pawn majority, whereas Black doesn't have even a trace of counterplay. } g6 8. b4 Kg7 9. Qb7 h5 10. h3 Kf6 11. Kg2 Rd6 12. a5 bxa5 13. bxa5 Re6 14. a6 Nc7 15. a7 Re7 16. Qc6+ $1 ( { Instead of giving Black some hopes of a fortress after } 16. Qxc7 $6 Rxc7 17. a8=Q $11 Kg7 { , Karpov goes for zugzwang. } ) 
Ke5 ( { More tenacious was } 16... Kg7 { intending } 17. Qd6 Re2 18. Kf3 Rc2 { as observed by hobbsey. Here White could try different ways to break Black's fortress, for example bringing the queen to c8 followed by a7-a8=Q at the right moment. } ) 
17. Kf3 { Seeing that any move loses material, Black resigned. } 
 1-0

